WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:17.383
LM: My name is Linet Mera.  I am a graduate student at UCSF, and today I am interviewing Anita Sil,

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:23.422
who is a professor at UCSF and is also an HHMI Early Career Investigator.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:28.662
and a mother of two kids, and a black belt in Tae Kwon Do.

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:36.969
LM: Tell me a little bit more about what it is like to be an HHMI Early Career Investigator,

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:42.775
and how is that different from just being an HHMI investigator in general?

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:47.046
AS: So the HHMI investigator program has been around for awhile,

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:52.318
and is generally for more established scientists.

00:00:52.319 --> 00:01:00.626
So a few years ago, HHMI decided that especially with the current state of funding,

00:01:00.627 --> 00:01:04.997
that it would be really... that they could have a big impact if they

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:09.702
decided to fund scientists at an earlier stage of their career.

00:01:09.703 --> 00:01:16.075
So they had their first Early Career Scientist competition in around 2009,

00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:22.215
and selected 50 people to be part of this program.

00:01:22.216 --> 00:01:28.321
It is a 6 year program with graduated levels of funding that increase over that 6 years.

00:01:28.322 --> 00:01:38.631
And unlike the investigator program, it is not a renewable program, but it's designed to give people a real boost

00:01:38.632 --> 00:01:42.335
at the time in their career that could have a lot of impact for them.

00:01:42.336 --> 00:01:47.139
LM: Let's talk about Tae Kwon Do. What is Tae Kwon Do all about,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.110
and how did you get into it? You know, what was the training like?

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:52.478
AS: Those are great questions.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:02:00.887
So formally Tae Kwon Do translates into something that means "The Way of the Hand and the Foot".

00:02:00.888 --> 00:02:07.493
And it is a martial art. It is a striking martial art that involves kicking--different types of kicks.

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:11.531
In my...there are some different styles of Tae Kwon Do

00:02:11.532 --> 00:02:16.702
and the one that I learned we also emphasized a lot of hand techniques.

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:24.677
And we used a sparring style that included both the hand techniques to the head

00:02:24.678 --> 00:02:32.084
as well as kicking techniques to the head, and other targets, other body targets. [

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:39.091
I really just fell into Tae Kwon Do by accident, really, so in the middle of graduate school,

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:42.695
I was looking around for a different workout.

00:02:42.696 --> 00:02:47.900
I was a graduate student at UCSF, and here at Milberry Union,

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:53.472
just across the street from my lab, in the gym, they were offering a Tae Kwon Do class.

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:58.444
For some reason I stuck with it even though

00:02:58.445 --> 00:03:05.618
I definitely didn't get into it with the idea of getting a black belt.

00:03:05.619 --> 00:03:08.221
And in fact if you had told me as a beginning student that

00:03:08.222 --> 00:03:13.860
I would one day earn a black belt and teach Tae Kwon Do, I would have laughed.

00:03:13.861 --> 00:03:18.798
I mean I would have not believed that that was even remotely possible.

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:24.904
But you know, we had a very supportive group of people.

00:03:24.905 --> 00:03:28.274
It was a UCSF studio, so we had some people from the community,

00:03:28.275 --> 00:03:35.781
but a lot of UCSF graduate students and medical students,

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:44.023
and so I guess in some ways it was kind of an environment of you know, nerdy, 20-40 year olds to begin with.

00:03:44.024 --> 00:03:50.763
And it wasn't a very testosterone driven environment.

00:03:50.764 --> 00:03:55.968
It was definitely an ambitious environment, but in the sense that

00:03:55.969 --> 00:04:05.645
people wanted to push students to the maximum but in a really positive kind of way.

00:04:05.646 --> 00:04:09.849
LM: How do you even get from starting out to black belt?

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.619
I am sure it is a little bit different in different dojos, or studios.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:21.827
In ours we had belt tests. That is probably fairly common in the Tae Kwon Do realm.

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:28.534
So there was kind of a progression from white belt to yellow belt, green belt, blue belt, red belt, and black belt.

00:04:28.535 --> 00:04:38.344
And I think a lot of people made it from white belt to sort of to green belt, kind of the intermediate stages,

00:04:38.345 --> 00:04:44.483
but then there was... So I think even for those people who came in with a lot of natural talent,

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:50.256
at some point in a martial art, you get to a stage where you are going to have to

00:04:50.257 --> 00:04:58.898
put a lot of work and effort in to move beyond that comfort zone and learn to do things that you,

00:04:58.899 --> 00:05:03.903
you know there is only so much that comes naturally before you have to put some amount of work into it.

00:05:03.904 --> 00:05:07.673
So we tended to lose people at that stage.

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:10.776
For me it took six years to go in between white belt and black belt,

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:16.282
and for other people it was a shorter time frame. And for others it was longer.

00:05:16.283 --> 00:05:20.820
So you didn't necessarily know, especially as a beginning student,

00:05:20.821 --> 00:05:26.526
when the next belt test was going to be because our belt tests were a long affair.

00:05:26.527 --> 00:05:34.133
So let's say, for an earlier belt, it was maybe a minimum of a four hour test,

00:05:34.134 --> 00:05:36.335
up through black belt, which is a two day test.

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:39.939
LM: Oh wow. AS: They are big time commitments from the instructors point of view,

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:45.711
so they weren't just, it was rarely just one person testing.

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:48.881
You would get to know beforehand when your belt test was going to be.

00:05:48.882 --> 00:05:53.519
Which was really important I think especially for the higher belts

00:05:53.520 --> 00:06:00.593
because for me and the two people from UCSF that I took my black belt test with,

00:06:00.594 --> 00:06:04.897
we trained for nine months, about 20 hours a week

00:06:04.898 --> 00:06:10.437
A lot of that training together, which is a real bonding experience for sure.

00:06:16.944 --> 00:06:23.282
LM: So what are the tests like? Do you just kick things? I don't know...what do they involve?

00:06:23.283 --> 00:06:25.751
AS: The nature of the test is really defined by the instructor.

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:32.191
And I had a few different instructors over the time that I was a Tae Kwon Do student,

00:06:32.192 --> 00:06:36.495
but the person who I think had the most influence on our tests

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:42.502
was the instructor that I had for the longest period of time, Philip Moore,

00:06:42.503 --> 00:06:45.671
who is now a professor at George Washington University.

00:06:45.672 --> 00:06:53.145
But he is great at pushing people to be the best that they can be.

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:57.717
And his general philosophy of testing was that

00:06:57.718 --> 00:07:02.655
if you push people to the point of mental and physical exhaustion,

00:07:02.656 --> 00:07:05.958
then you can see what they are really capable of doing.

00:07:05.959 --> 00:07:12.265
So if you are fresh, there is a number of kicks or techniques that of course you can do well,

00:07:12.266 --> 00:07:16.035
but if you really know how to do them, then you should be able to do them at

00:07:16.036 --> 00:07:21.741
the end of two days of physical hardship, I think was the idea.

00:07:21.742 --> 00:07:25.244
So our tests would include running. We would run sets of stairs.

00:07:25.245 --> 00:07:32.552
There's two hundred steps between Kirkham and Lawton street in San Francisco.

00:07:32.553 --> 00:07:38.424
On 15th Avenue, there's a steep set of stairs outside so we would run to those stairs.

00:07:38.425 --> 00:07:45.765
We would have to run there holding some of those big exercise balls from the gym.

00:07:45.766 --> 00:07:48.701
We would run sets of stairs. We would row.

00:07:48.702 --> 00:07:53.706
We would of course do a lot of kicking techniques and hand techniques,

00:07:53.707 --> 00:07:57.143
and sparring as a component of the tests.

00:07:57.144 --> 00:07:58.611
We would do board breaks.

00:07:58.612 --> 00:08:05.117
Formal techniques where there are...

00:08:05.118 --> 00:08:14.427
so Tae Kwon Do has specific forms or kata, where you do sort of a set of techniques.

00:08:14.428 --> 00:08:22.468
It's a group of techniquest that you do together, like a set of kicks or hand techniques.

00:08:22.469 --> 00:08:30.042
And they get progressively more complicated as you move up and learn different types of techniques.

00:08:30.043 --> 00:08:34.313
LM: So you spoke a little bit about how to manage your schedule.

00:08:34.314 --> 00:08:37.517
Can you talk about how you've juggled all of those things?

00:08:37.518 --> 00:08:40.119
AS: Yeah, I think the juggling is a challenge.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:47.527
I always smile internally when people call it balance because it doesn't feel like balance in any kind of way.

00:08:47.528 --> 00:08:55.168
I think a lot of it is compartmentalizing different things at different times.

00:08:55.169 --> 00:08:59.572
So when I have to... when I know I am going to have to go home and

00:08:59.573 --> 00:09:03.843
make 30 cupcakes because my son is celebrating his birthday

00:09:03.844 --> 00:09:07.280
in class the next day, I don't worry about it when I am at work.

00:09:07.281 --> 00:09:10.516
I am like, OK, I am going to deal with that later.

00:09:10.517 --> 00:09:13.519
And when one of my kids is sick, and my husband is with them,

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:18.357
and I can't be there, and I am at work, I try not to think about that either.

00:09:18.358 --> 00:09:27.166
I basically have to be very organized and focus on whatever is the task at hand.

00:09:27.167 --> 00:09:29.836
and get it done.

00:09:29.837 --> 00:09:37.243
There's... I think a lot of it is about figuring out how to be really efficient

00:09:37.244 --> 00:09:42.114
and what types of things... you know what can I do when I am tired?

00:09:42.115 --> 00:09:43.916
And do that then.

00:09:43.917 --> 00:09:46.752
What do I have to be really on the ball to get done?

00:09:46.753 --> 00:09:53.359
And sort of figure out times of the day that maximize my productivity.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:10:00.299
LM: So do you see a lot of parallels or connections between your training and your scientific career?

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:06.539
AS: I think they definitely influence each other, probably in ways that I didn't fully appreciate at the time.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:13.379
They are very similar journeys where there is a big picture goal at the end of it,

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:16.682
but to get there you have to focus on the day to day

00:10:16.683 --> 00:10:23.289
and lose some of the anxiety about is this ultimately going to add up to something.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:24.657
Am I ever going to graduate?

00:10:24.658 --> 00:10:28.027
Am I ever going to get this side kick to work?

00:10:28.028 --> 00:10:34.133
You just sort of have some faith that, at least,

00:10:34.134 --> 00:10:38.504
I am going to give it the best that I can and I am going to take it one step at a time.

00:10:38.505 --> 00:10:45.846
And before you know it you've actually gotten somewhere that you might not have imagined you were capable of.

